  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will move through the region this morning, with \ndecreasing humidity levels expected in its wake for this \nafternoon as dewpoints drop into the 60s for most areas, with \neven some 50s possible across portions of the SW Adirondacks and\nMohawk Valley by late this afternoon. So, despite similar \nmaximum temperatures to today (80s in valleys and upper \n70s/lower 80s for higher terrain), max heat indices will be even\nless, mainly in the mid 80s/lower 90s in valley areas. It will \nalso become breezy this afternoon in the wake of the front, \nwith west/northwest wind potentially gusting up to 20-25 mph at \ntimes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWeather pattern becomes more active this week as broad upper\nlevel troughing develops across the Great Lakes/Northeast and \nSE Canada. Strengthening mid/upper level winds (H500 winds 30-50\nKT) on the south/southwest periphery of the developing trough \nwill enhance the potential for stronger thunderstorms with any \ndisturbance that passes through, especially should they track \nfarther north allowing for pockets of greater instability to \ndevelop across our region. \n\nOne disturbance looks to pass through Monday afternoon/evening.\nLatest SPC HREFs suggest probs for SB CAPES >1000 J/kg at \n60-80%+ for the most of Mohawk Valley and Hudson River Valley \n(mainly from Albany south) amid 30-35 KT 0-6 km shear. So there \nis some severe potential, with strong/damaging wind gusts the \nmain hazard expected during the afternoon/evening hours. \nAlthough storms should be moving fairly quickly, there could be \nsome brief backbuilding/training of cells especially across the \nSE Catskills/mid Hudson Valley region, where SPC HREF indicates \nthe prob for >3\" rainfall in 3 hours at 10-30%, so isolated \nflash flooding can not be ruled out where any training cells \noccur.\n\nIn the wake of the Monday system, another potent system looks to\ntrack across late Wednesday or Thursday. Strong winds aloft\n(H500 hPa winds 45-55 KT) could promote strong/damaging winds\nwithin any convection. The only limiting factor could be\ninstability depending on air mass recovery during this time,\nhowever given strong forcing combined with these wind fields\naloft, there could still be low-topped convection containing\nstrong/damaging winds. Will need to watch trends closely with\nthe late week system for severe potential.",
